# PHASE 4 – INTELLIGENT TECHNICIAN MATCHING & AUTOMATIC ASSIGNMENT

## Objective

Develop the core intelligent assignment engine.

The system must automatically find the most suitable technician for every accepted booking.

Do not simply select the nearest technician.

Consider:
- Skills.
- Certifications.
- Availability.
- Location.
- Travel time.
- Workload.
- Existing assignments.
- Customer preferences.
- Job priority.
- Appointment deadlines.

## 4.1 Job Processing

For every booking, retrieve:

- Required service.
- Required skills.
- Required certifications.
- Customer location.
- Preferred appointment window.
- Estimated duration.
- Verified priority.

Convert accepted bookings into scheduling-ready jobs.

## 4.2 Priority Queue

Implement a priority-based job queue.

Consider:

- Verified emergency status.
- Job priority.
- SLA deadline.
- Waiting time.
- Aging to prevent starvation.

Emergency jobs receive higher consideration but must still satisfy mandatory scheduling constraints.

Do not trust customer-selected emergency priority automatically.

## 4.3 Technician Eligibility Filtering

Filter technicians based on:

- Approval status.
- Mandatory skills.
- Certifications.
- Service area.
- Working hours.
- Availability.
- Existing bookings.
- Schedule conflicts.
- Travel feasibility.
- Deadline feasibility.

Exclude any technician who violates a mandatory constraint.

## 4.4 Dijkstra Travel-Time Calculation

Represent the road network as a weighted graph:

- Nodes represent locations.
- Edges represent roads.
- Edge weights represent travel time.

Implement Dijkstra's algorithm to calculate shortest travel-time paths.

Calculate travel times for eligible technicians.

Do not use straight-line distance as a substitute for actual road-network routing.

## 4.5 Customer Technician Preferences

Allow customers to optionally request a preferred technician.

Rules:

- Honour the preference if the technician satisfies all mandatory constraints.
- If not feasible, explain why.
- Offer alternative technicians or appointment options.
- Never allow Round Robin to override a valid customer preference.

## 4.6 Assignment Scoring

For jobs without a valid technician preference, implement:

Assignment Cost =

(w1 × Travel Cost)

+ (w2 × SLA Risk)

+ (w3 × Workload Imbalance)

+ (w4 × Schedule Disruption Cost)

Use configurable and documented weights.

Choose the feasible technician with the lowest assignment cost.

Scoring must never override hard constraints.

## 4.7 Round Robin Fairness

Use Round Robin only as a tie-breaker among similarly suitable candidates.

Consider:

- Number of assigned jobs.
- Total assigned working hours.
- Recent workload.
- Existing schedule commitments.

Do not assign an unsuitable technician simply because it is their turn.

## 4.8 Safe Assignment

Before committing:

- Revalidate availability.
- Check overlapping jobs.
- Verify skills.
- Verify appointment feasibility.
- Use database transactions.
- Implement schedule versioning or locking.
- Prevent duplicate assignments.

If validation fails, retry or return the job to the unassigned queue.

## 4.9 Explainability

Record:

- Assigned technician.
- Booking ID.
- Assignment timestamp.
- Estimated travel time.
- Assignment score.
- Selection reason.
- Rejected alternatives.
- Rejection reasons.
- Scheduling run ID.

Provide human-readable explanations to dispatchers.

## 4.10 Testing

Test:
- Correct skill match.
- Missing skill.
- Missing certification.
- Unapproved technician.
- Technician unavailable.
- Overlapping assignment.
- Out-of-zone technician.
- Customer technician preference.
- Emergency priority.
- No eligible technician.
- Concurrent scheduling requests.

**Deliverable:** Working intelligent technician matching and automatic assignment engine.

